---
/**
 * The masthead. Lives inside the dark band declared by Base.astro's `heroBand`,
 * so every token here resolves to the charcoal palette in both themes.
 *
 * The portrait bleeds in from the left and the type column sits on the right.
 * The photo's own studio backdrop is nearly the same charcoal as the band, so it
 * dissolves into it rather than sitting on it as a picture.
 *
 * Note on the type: the font-size lives on child <span>s, never on the <h1>.
 * global.css forces `h1 { text-2xl !important }` and a utility class cannot beat
 * it; a child inherits and wins with no !important at all. See DESIGN.md §3.4.
 */
import ButtonLink from "@/components/ui/ButtonLink.astro";
import HandRule from "@/components/ui/HandRule.astro";
import { currentPosition } from "@/data/career";
import portrait from "@/assets/images/portrait.webp";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";

const current = currentPosition();
const role = current.roles[0]?.title ?? "Web Tech Lead";

const stack = [
	"TypeScript",
	"Node.js",
	"Java",
	"Spring Boot",
	"PostgreSQL",
	"React",
	"Redux",
	"MapLibre",
	"deck.gl",
	"WebGL",
	"three.js",
	"Git",
];

// The wordmark scales with viewport WIDTH, but the space it has to fit into is
// bounded by viewport HEIGHT: on a wide, short screen (1440x800 and friends) the
// type ran big enough to push the tech stack line below the fold. The `min()`
// with a vh term caps it on short viewports.
const wordmark =
	"block font-heading uppercase leading-[0.84] tracking-[-0.02em] text-[clamp(3rem,min(9.5vw,11vh),9.5rem)]";
---

<section class="relative flex flex-1 flex-col pb-8 pt-4 md:pb-8 md:pt-5">
	{/* Full-bleed portrait, anchored left. In flow on mobile, absolute on desktop —
		the same element either way, so the browser only ever fetches one image.
		Text over the portrait was unreadable on a phone: the face landed right
		behind the wordmark and the role line. */}
	<div
		aria-hidden="true"
		class="pointer-events-none relative left-1/2 -ml-[50vw] mb-7 h-[32vh] min-h-[12rem] w-screen md:absolute md:inset-y-0 md:mb-0 md:h-auto md:min-h-0"
	>
		{/* The cap is the source image's own width, 1024px, not a guessed value.
			It was 38rem (608px), which threw away real photograph: with object-cover
			and object-position 30%, 70% of the horizontal overflow is cropped from
			the RIGHT, so at any viewport from 1280px up exactly 160px of the frame's
			right side was being discarded — while the photo simultaneously ended
			288px short of the text column at 1920px, leaving a dead strip and a hard
			vertical edge.

			At 64rem the frame is 48vw until 2133px, where it meets the source
			resolution; past that it stops growing rather than upscaling a 1024px
			image into a blur. The right crop falls to zero by 1920px. */}
		<div
			class="relative h-full w-full overflow-hidden md:w-[48%] md:max-w-[64rem]"
		>
			{/* The source is square with a lot of empty studio backdrop, so a landscape
				mobile crop leaves the figure small. Scaling from the top zooms in
				without cutting the head. */}
			{/* fetchpriority="high" because this is the page's LCP element. `eager`
				only opts it out of lazy loading; it still queues at the browser's
				default image priority, behind the stylesheet and scripts. PageSpeed
				flags exactly this as "LCP request discovery": the image is found in
				the initial HTML, it just is not asked for urgently. */}
			<Image
				src={portrait}
				alt=""
				widths={[520, 760, 1024]}
				sizes="(min-width: 768px) 48vw, 100vw"
				loading="eager"
				decoding="sync"
				fetchpriority="high"
				class="h-full w-full origin-top scale-[1.35] object-cover object-[50%_10%] md:scale-100 md:object-[30%_20%]"
			/>
			{/* Mobile: only the bottom edge dissolves into the band, so the portrait
				stays legible instead of sitting under a heavy scrim. */}
			<div
				class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-b from-transparent from-55% to-bgColor md:hidden"
			>
			</div>
			{/* Desktop: fade the right edge into the band so the photo doesn't end on a
				hard vertical seam.

				The stops used to start at 55%, which was measured wrong rather than
				chosen. A luminance scan across the rendered frame puts the subject
				between roughly 30% and 81% of its width, peaking at 60-65% — so the
				veil began in the brightest part of the torso and took the arm, the
				stool and the legs with it.

				Two things make a late start safe. The text column never reaches further
				left than 92% of the frame at any viewport (measured 92-95% from 768px
				to 1280px, and past the frame entirely from 1440px up), so it is not
				sitting on the photo. And the photo's own studio backdrop is within a
				few levels of the band colour by 85% across, so there is very little
				left to hide.

				The stops step up with the breakpoint because the geometry genuinely
				changes: the frame is min(48vw, 38rem) but its height is the whole
				band, so a wider viewport crops less and pushes the subject right, and
				past 1400px the text stops overlapping the frame at all.

				From 1400px up the gradient is therefore only softening an edge, and it
				changes shape as well as position. The `via` alpha is set to exactly
				the value a straight line would pass through at that stop (40% at 93%,
				between 0% at 86% and 80% at 100%), which removes the kink the other
				breakpoints have and makes the fade perfectly even — the most gradual
				curve there is.

				It also stops at 80% rather than reaching the full band colour. The
				photo's own studio backdrop measures luma 17 against the band's 23, so
				an 80% veil composites to 21.8: a difference of about one level in 255,
				which no one can see. Spending the last 20% of opacity to close a gap
				that isn't visible only steepens the ramp. */}
			<div
				class="absolute inset-0 hidden bg-gradient-to-r from-transparent from-[76%] via-bgColor/55 via-[90%] to-bgColor md:block xl:from-[82%] xl:via-[92%] min-[1400px]:from-[84%] min-[1400px]:via-bgColor/40 min-[1400px]:via-[92%] min-[1400px]:to-bgColor/80"
			>
			</div>
			{/* Feather the top and bottom edges into the band — but ONLY the edges.
				This used to be one full-height gradient,
				`from-bgColor via-transparent to-bgColor/70`, which put a 70%-opaque
				charcoal veil on the top of the frame ramping to transparent at the
				vertical middle. The subject's head sits around 20% from the top, so
				it was carrying roughly 42% charcoal: a visible black halo that
				swallowed the hair and flattened the face.

				Two short gradients do the actual job the veil was there for — hiding
				the hard top and bottom edges behind the header and the tech-stack
				line — and leave everything between them untouched. */}
			<div
				class="absolute inset-x-0 top-0 hidden h-24 bg-gradient-to-b from-bgColor to-transparent md:block"
			>
			</div>
			<div
				class="absolute inset-x-0 bottom-0 hidden h-40 bg-gradient-to-t from-bgColor to-transparent md:block"
			>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	{/* flex-1 + justify-center so the content sits in the middle of the
		full-height band, with the stack line pinned below it. */}
	<div
		class="relative flex flex-1 flex-col justify-center md:ml-auto md:w-[56%] md:max-w-[40rem]"
	>
		{/* `md:shrink-0` used to sit on this label so it wouldn't compress. At
			exactly the md breakpoint the column is ~430px wide and the label needs
			510px, so instead of compressing it overflowed the page by 84px and the
			text was clipped mid-word ("…BUILDING FOR THE WEB SI"), invisible
			because <html> carries overflow-x-hidden. Letting it wrap is the whole
			fix; `text-balance` keeps the two lines even. */}
		<div data-reveal class="flex items-center gap-5">
			<p
				class="text-balance font-mono text-[0.6875rem] uppercase leading-relaxed tracking-[0.2em] text-color-550 md:text-xs"
			>
				01 — Galicia, Spain · Building for the web since 2006
			</p>
			<HandRule class="hidden flex-1" />
		</div>

		<h1 class="display-heading mt-5 md:mt-6">
			<span
				data-reveal="rise"
				style="--reveal-delay:40ms"
				class:list={[wordmark, "font-medium text-accent-base/40"]}>Fran</span
			>
			<span
				data-reveal="rise"
				style="--reveal-delay:80ms"
				class:list={[wordmark, "font-bold text-accent-base"]}>Dieguez</span
			>
		</h1>

		<p
			data-reveal
			style="--reveal-delay:140ms"
			class="mt-6 font-mono text-[0.8125rem] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent-two md:mt-7 md:text-sm"
		>
			{role} · open sourcerer · full stack developer
		</p>

		<p
			data-reveal="rise"
			style="--reveal-delay:180ms"
			class="mt-5 max-w-[42ch] text-xl leading-[1.5]"
		>
			I'm a full stack developer and {role} at {current.org}, where we do indoor
			positioning for people and robots. I build{" "}
			<span
				class="relative isolate inline-block before:absolute before:inset-x-[-0.2em] before:inset-y-[0.14em] before:-z-10 before:-rotate-[1.2deg] before:rounded-[3px] before:bg-accent-two/25"
				>the visual layer</span
			> — map viewers, 3D layers, the code that turns complex spatial data into
			something fast enough to think with.
		</p>

		<div data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:220ms" class="mt-8 flex flex-col gap-6">
			<div class="flex flex-col items-start gap-4">
				<div class="flex flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:gap-4">
					<ButtonLink href="/posts/" size="lg">Read the blog</ButtonLink>
					<ButtonLink href="/contact/" variant="secondary" size="lg">
						➤ Get in touch
					</ButtonLink>
				</div>
				<a
					href="/about/"
					class="citrus-link group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-accent-two"
				>
					The long version
					<span
						aria-hidden="true"
						class="transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1">→</span
					>
				</a>
			</div>
			<p class="max-w-[30ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-color-600">
				Open to collaborating on things that are genuinely interesting.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>

	<p
		data-reveal
		style="--reveal-delay:260ms"
		class="relative mt-8 font-mono text-[0.6875rem] uppercase leading-relaxed tracking-[0.16em] text-color-550 md:mt-8 md:text-xs"
	>
		{stack.join(" · ")}
	</p>
</section>
